Fillets: of two roundings overlapping on a face, the one whose contact
lies nearer its edge rides (0016), and a ride of no width drops the rider
(mitcad#1533).

The roof slab's eaves rounded with 0.65 to 0.87: the tip's and the obtuse
edge's roundings overlap on the eave. The stripes' order chose the rider:
on the left eave the obtuse edge rode on the tip's line, on the right
eave the tip on the obtuse edge's, a shape of its own, which jumped by
2.4 in volume where the tip's rounding begins to roll (1650). Just below
that (0.862 to 0.868) the left obtuse edge's ride, its section narrower
than 0.012, failed in its walk, and 1640 took the ride the other way
round: the volume jumped by 2.

- Compute (the rides of overlapping stripes): the stripe whose contact on
  the face lies nearer its own edge (r / tan(a / 2), a the angle of its
  faces on the ball's side, contactReach) rides; as the radius grows the
  other's contact reaches that edge and the other then rolls on it (1650).
  Equal ones keep their order.
- Where that ride fails and the other's contact line lies within a
  fiftieth of the rider's radius of the rider's edge all along
  (rideOfNoWidth), the rider takes (nearly) nothing and is dropped with
  its corners (as 1650 drops it once the other rolls on its edge), not
  ridden on the other way round.

